WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.969
Film Courage: Does a network require that
you submit an outline before writing?

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:05.569
Christine Conradt: Sometimes.

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:08.870
It depends on the exec that you’re working
with and it depends on the producer.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.130
Sometimes the producer will require it.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:16.520
Sometimes it’s just we go from one paragraph
to one page.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.930
Sometimes we go from one paragraph to a script,
whatever they want, whatever makes them feel

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.000
comfortable in their process is what I deliver
to them.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.360
Film Courage: What’s the typical show that
you’re writing for, the length of time?

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.349
Christine Conradt: That is takes me to write
it?

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:33.700
Film Courage: What would be the typical air-time?

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:40.840
Christine Conradt: For most television, it’s
83 to 86 minutes for a feature.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.360
Film Courage: Then what does that translate
into in terms of pages?

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.800
Christine Conradt: It depends on the producer
in terms of pages.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.720
So you know sort of the standard is one page
equals one minute of screen time.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.530
That’s sort of a loose guide.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:03.450
I have a producer I work with who won’t
shoot anything under 116 pages because he

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:09.830
likes to have a lot of footage to cut down
to help the pacing.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:15.570
I have other producers I work with who they
don’t want anything over a hundred pages.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:19.670
So it depends on their production process.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.910
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.840
So I’m really getting the sense that it’s
all about flexibility.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.550
Christine Conradt: It is all about flexibility.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:29.550
Yes!

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.409
Being flexible is definitely a help.

00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:34.570
And again, giving them what they want.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.710
When I ask producers “Why do you fire writers?”

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.140
I ask every producer I’ve worked with “Why
do you fire writers?”

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.190
And they will always say “Because they don’t
give me what I ask them to give me.”

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:47.190
So either notes they’ve given and the writer
comes back and they haven’t done that with

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.640
the notes because they thought they knew better
or they just didn’t know how.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.570
Or they asked for a certain page count and
it wasn’t that page count.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:58.330
To me, those are the wrong reasons to get
fired off of a project.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:01:59.799
Those are things you can control.

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:04.350
So if you’re just paying attention and asking
them what they want and they want to tell

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.630
you what they want.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.200
I ask “What do you want your page lengths
to be here?

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.780
Where do you want your act lengths to be?”

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.950
And then that’s what I give them.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:19.150
Film Courage: So what’s the time frame that
you’re usually given?

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.640
Once they’ve given you the title, you’ve
met with them, you’ve come up with some

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.600
ideas, what time frame are you working with?

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.840
Christine Conradt: It depends.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:33.310
Typically I get 4 weeks for a first draft
and then two weeks for every subsequent draft,

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.170
which is pretty quick actually.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:40.599
It’s not that difficult if you’re only
working on one project at a time but if you’re

00:02:40.599 --> 00:02:46.319
a working writer you’re working on multiple
projects because it takes so long before one

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:48.750
of them suddenly comes back and says “Okay,
we’re greenlit.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.250
We can actually move forward.”

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.290
You don’t know when that’s going to happen.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:57.150
So you’ve got all these sort of irons in
the fire and then eventually one will come

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.290
back and sometimes two come back, which is
great.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:04.209
But now you have to balance how you are going
to deliver both of those scripts.

